<p>Thank you for taking a look! Response below.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I have no <em>specific</em> narrative planned at the moment. What I have is just beats I wanted to hit and concepts to play with: the &quot;Home Sweet Home&quot; sign and the out-of-place doll suggests a potential story about someone without a home finding one in the doll house.</p>
<p>I imagine a D-Class or researcher who never really knew her/his/their family possessing the doll and not wanting to leave the house, the narrative borne out chiefly through radio communication logs (perhaps through a toy walkie-talkie placed in the house that then turns into a high-quality two-way radio from the subject possessing the doll's perspective) and/or journal entries. Perhaps the D-Class's description of the place and how nice it is makes a researcher jealous and they end up switching places with them without permission? Though I don't know how they'd think they'd get away with that for long&#8230;</p>
<p>Then maybe the research team purposely switches to a test subject that had a happier home life or whose psychological profile suggests they'd get bored or feel trapped. They reason that test subject would eventually want to leave and explore outside the house&#8230;</p>
<p>Perhaps, actually, it would be best to get rid of the possibility of switching out test subjects, and just have enough evidence discovered beforehand for them to know the best way to set up an experiment. The person that goes in is stuck there until they die walking through the portal. This could lay the narrative on a single character, and prevent the research team from simply changing test subjects before they get too comfortable inside&#8230;</p>
<p>Sorry, I guess this turned into more brainstorming than anything. I know I haven't given a lot of specifics, but does any of this feel like a step in the right direction or have I still not provided enough to work with? (I imagine the latter. Sorry if I'm wasting your time.)</p>
</div>
